<!--
    An indexed store into a stack array that may overwrite a slot written
    earlier: a later read of that slot must see the store.  Before this
    test, the slot's earlier value was forwarded past the store, so the
    test or result printed the stale value (`if ((char)a0)` where the
    binary re-reads b[0] after `b[(i >> 4) % 12] = j`; for i = 5, j = 0
    the binary returns 5 and the printed C returns 6).

    The code is x86-64, byte for byte at its link address
    (-nostdlib -static -Ttext=0x401000, -fno-stack-protector
    -fcf-protection=none): g_* are gcc 11 -O2, c_* the same source with
    clang 14 -O2, o_* gcc 11 -O0.

        #define U(i) ((unsigned)(i))
        int byte(int i, int j) { unsigned char b[12]; b[0] = i;
            b[(U(i) >> 4) % 12] = j; if (b[0]) return b[0] + 1; return 5; }
        int int_(...)   the same with int b[12]
        long long_(...) the same with long b[12]
        int struct_(int i, int j) { struct S { int a; int b; } s[6];
            s[0].a = i; s[0].b = j; s[(U(i) >> 4) % 6].a = j;
            return s[0].a * 3 + s[0].b; }
        int second(int i, int j) { int b[8]; b[1] = i;
            b[U(j) % 8] = i + j; return b[1] - 7; }
        int two(int i, int j) { short b[10]; b[0] = i; b[3] = j;
            b[(U(i) + U(j)) % 10] = 77; return b[0] + b[3] * 2; }

    s[0].b is never reachable by the store (it writes only .a fields), so
    struct_ still adds the parameter j.

    gb_* (gcc 11 -O2) and cb_* (clang 14 -O2) at 0x402000 read a slot past
    the four elements the stack layout gives an array whose window value-set
    analysis did not lock; the index's own bound reaches it:

        int ib(int i, int j) { int b[12]; b[0] = i; b[5] = j;
            b[(U(i) >> 4) % 12] = 7; return b[0] + b[5] * 3; }
        int im(int i, int j) { int b[8]; b[1] = i; b[6] = j;
            b[U(j) & 7] = 9; return b[1] - b[6]; }
        int sb(int i, int j) { short b[10]; b[0] = i; b[7] = j;
            b[(U(i) + U(j)) % 10] = 77; return b[0] + b[7] * 5; }
        int cb(int i, int j) { unsigned char b[12]; b[0] = i; b[9] = j;
            b[(U(i) >> 4) % 12] = 5; return b[0] + b[9] * 3; }
        int l6b(int i, int j) { long b[8]; b[6] = i; b[1] = j; b[j & 7] = 3;
            return (int)(b[6] + b[1]); }
        int u2(int i, int j) { long b[8]; b[1] = i; b[2] = i + 1; b[j] = 3;
            return (int)(b[1] + b[2]); }
        int u5(int i, int j) { int b[8]; b[1] = i; b[j] = 3; return (short)b[1]; }

    u2 and u5 index with no bound: the guarded b[1] and b[2] lie inside the
    four elements the layout gives the array, and a narrower read of them
    (`(int)b[1]`, `(short)b[1]`) must not split the array into scalars, or
    the C would store through `(&v1)[a1]` past a scalar or with the wrong
    stride.

    Stage: P3 heritage, stackstoreguard: an indexed stack STORE of any
    width gets an INDIRECT on each written stack range its analyzed window
    may reach.  P0 assertion: option stackstoreguard on|off.  Pass 1
    (default on) proves the fix: every read after the indexed store is a
    read of the array.  Pass 2 (off) reproduces the stale reads.  On the
    unpatched engine #1 to #4, #9, #11 and #13 count 0 and #5 to #8, #10 and
    #12 count twice their value.
-->
